<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ram Raja Temple</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ram Raja Temple is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple in Orchha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, where the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> is worshipped as a reigning king. According to tradition, Queen Ganesh Kunwari of Orchha brought an image of Rama from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> in the 16th century, and it could not be moved from the palace where it was first placed. Rama is honoured there with a daily armed salute, and the temple draws large numbers of pilgrims.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ram-raja-temple.jpg" alt="Ram Raja Temple" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ram_Raja_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
